    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a loft addition for a residential project up in Oregon. I'm looking at using a Douglas Fir beam for the main span. The span is 14 feet and I'm expecting a dead load of about 15 psf plus a live load of 30 psf. Can you help me figure out what size beam I'll need to keep deflection within L/360 limits?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Given a 14-foot span for your Douglas Fir beam in Oregon, considering a dead load of 15 psf and a live load of 30 psf, you need to account for both. Using the dead load only is optimized for lowest weight for a given span, but safety concerns usually require accounting for both loads together in design. An initial check in a table or using software like LCC-BBTE or ICC-ES's Range Equation with appropriate modification factors is required to determine the right beam size and ensure deflection limit compliance. The expected deflection limit in this context seems to concern whether the beam will meet the American Iron and Steel Institute (AISC) specifications of L/360 for live loads alone. For Oregon and beyond, conditions such as temperature, humidity, and duration of live load also factor into this assessment. It would be wise to consult with a structural engineer for precise calculations.",
      "source": "target",
